d}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=4,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416597 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416597 #edb/ref 1069 "q-113526" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416597 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416597 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $B={[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}$  be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416597 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4f239f11-8e8b-53a2-a96f-22cc233efad1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416597 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $S$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{e}_{1},{e}_{2}}$, respectively. Recall that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $S$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {e}_{1} & {e}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-6){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{S\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416598 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416598 #edb/ref 1069 "q-113508" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416598 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416598 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $B={[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}$ and $C={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416598 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "699ab275-8547-554b-97d2-1ddcd2846071" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416598 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 &